/P 588 0 R
/E (Two areas of concern happened to be in the bathroom.  There was a fairly wide gap on the floor below the S facing wall,  and below the E facing wall.  So after careful sweeping,  I made 5 pieces of tin from roof flashing that I had on hand.  These were screwed down to secure.  \(We at first thought it was possible to crawl  underneath the warm-up shelter to see if any tin had come down allowing mice entry,  but that idea quickly went away!\))
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
942 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 588 0 R
/E (Next, I tried to replace the red outside  bulbs on the N  of the building which let people know that the site is in use, but I couldn't get them out.  I will look at that again later on with another removal method.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
943 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 588 0 R
/E (Then I applied some plumbers putty sealant to the entry points of the power to the equipment shed, S side.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
944 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 588 0 R
/E (Now while I was doing all of this, John pruned back the thicker weeds/trees around and inside the U. Of S. Compound and sprayed round-up on the weeds.  He also assisted me where necessary.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
945 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 588 0 R
/E (One thing we made note of was that we need to haul out all the wood from under the warm-up shelter in spring. Any good pieces can be kept in our maintenance shed.  )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
946 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 588 0 R
/E (So I would say that we had a good day.  I want to thank John for all his help.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
